  "narration": [
    {
      "t": 0.5,
      "text": "A rollout sheet can promise two completely different things, and both of them render cleanly."
    },
    {
      "t": 4.8,
      "text": "Three units here, spread over two waves."
    },
    {
      "t": 10.8,
      "text": "Render the sheet the obvious way. It is happy, and it gives us an id for what it made."
    },
    {
      "t": 16.8,
      "text": "But read the gate lines. Nothing is waiting for anything, this sheet sequences nothing at all."
    },
    {
      "t": 22.8,
      "text": "Render it again, this time asking for the waves to be locked together. Different id already."
    },
    {
      "t": 28.8,
      "text": "Now the first wave starts and the second one waits for it. That is the sheet we wanted."
    },
    {
      "t": 34.8,
      "text": "Two renderings on record, and nothing released yet."
    },
    {
      "t": 38.5,
      "text": "Without that switch the waves only look ordered. With it, each one waits for the one before."
    }
  ],
  "notes": "Teaches that rendering only sequences the waves when the interlock switch is passed, and shows the parallel gates it writes otherwise."
}
